task: lower-maxdepth-real-margin
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/lower-maxdepth-real-margin
worktree: ../okay-wt-lowerdepth
spec: specs/codecs.md ("The margin, measured" — the two roads it named out)

why: the operator's step 1 of a two-step plan (step 2 is making the
  recursive decoders iterative, specs entry to follow separately).
  stack-depth-margin measured, not chose: on a JVM thread with the
  DEFAULT 1MB stack, a full-depth (256) decode of a recursive schema
  needs the whole 1024 KB, leaving no real margin for the caller. 256
  was inherited from Cbor.maxSkipDepth and justified by precedent
  (serde_json 128, Jackson 1000), not by what THIS module's frames
  cost.

what: lower Codecs.maxDepth from 256 to a number picked from the
  measured per-level cost (~8 KB/tree-level via the interpreted fold,
  the worst measured door), verified by re-running TestStackBytes at
  the new value rather than assumed from linear extrapolation. Target:
  the worst door's stack cost at the new limit should leave real room
  on a default 1 MB thread (a quarter or less of it, so the caller
  keeps most of the stack). Update every place that states 256 in
  prose (specs/codecs.md, docs/modules/okay-codec.md, CHANGELOG) to
  the new number and the reasoning, and the tests that hardcode 256
  as a literal rather than reading Codecs.maxDepth.

predict: ~64 fits the target (a quarter of 256 in KB terms too, so a
  linear relationship should hold and TestStackBytes should confirm
  ~256 KB for the worst door) — MEASURE this rather than trust the
  arithmetic, per verify-assumptions-before-acting.
